    <li><strong>Basic I/O functions</strong> (printf, scanf, getch, gets, putchar) — high-frequency across all years.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>5.2 Allocate Time Proportionally to Question Type Weights</h3>
  <p>Since 2021, the exam has settled into a 45.5% MCQ / 45.5% Short / 9.1% Long split. Preparation time should reflect this: roughly equal emphasis on objective (MCQ) and short-answer practice, with focused long-answer preparation for the three essay-style questions.</p>
  <h3>5.3 Strengthen Operator and Expression Skills</h3>
  <p>The rising trend in operator-related questions (1–2 per year in 2015–2016 → 6–7 per year in 2023–2025) signals a curricular shift. Students should thoroughly understand arithmetic, logical, relational, assignment, increment/decrement, and bitwise operators, as well as operator precedence.</p>
  <h3>5.4 Database Theory Over Query Practice</h3>
  <p>The exam is heavily theory-oriented in the database section: definitions of keys (primary, foreign, candidate, super, composite), relationships (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many), and data models (hierarchical, network, relational, object-oriented) dominate. Actual SQL query writing is rare. Curriculum planners should ensure conceptual clarity takes precedence over complex query syntax.</p>
  <h3>5.5 Do Not Over-Invest in File Handling</h3>
  <p>With only 6 questions across 10 years (0.6 per year on average), file handling modes (r, w, a) and functions (fopen, fclose) are low-yield topics. A brief overview suffices.</p>
  <h3>5.6 Leverage the Invariant Long Question Structure</h3>
  <p>Exactly 3 Long Questions appear every year, typically covering: (a) control structures or loops, (b) database concepts or data models, and (c) functions or C fundamentals. Teachers can build predictable practice templates around these three pillars.</p>
</section>

